[72.146904, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[72.197657, "o", "The TSG Helpdesk on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, setup: first-draft   (fictional AnyCompany, Region us-east-1)\r\n\r\n  piece             its job                                        what this setup has\r\n  Runtime           runs the agent code: one microVM per session   tsg_helpdesk, PYTHON_3_13, network PUBLIC, inbound IAM SigV4\r\n                                                                   (the default)\r\n  Identity          who may call; whose tokens the agent uses      workload identity tsg_helpdesk-AbCdEf1234; vault provider\r\n                                                                   tickets-saas (USER_FEDERATION)\r\n"]
[72.19789, "o", "  Gateway           one MCP door to the tools                      tsg-helpdesk-gw, 3 tools, MCP, inbound CUSTOM_JWT\r\n                                                                   (allowedScopes)\r\n  Policy            a yes or no on every tool call                 2 Cedar policies, mode LOG_ONLY\r\n  Memory            what the agent remembers, per user             tsg_helpdesk_mem, namespace /helpdesk/facts/\r\n  Code Interpreter  runs code in a sandbox                         PUBLIC network, role role/tsg-code-interpreter-role\r\n  Browser           a cloud web browser                            none\r\n  Observability     the record of what happened                    data events: none; tracing: runtime\r\n  Evaluations       scores answers after release                   none\r\n\r\nOn Lambda or ECS you would build each of these yourself. Here each one is a separate AWS service with its own\r\nsettings, its own IAM actions and its own logs. Files: setups/first-draft/*.json and policies/*.cedar\r\n"]
[72.20599, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b]0;thesecguy@lab: ~/ai-security-course-labs/labs/m14\u0007\u001b[01;32mthesecguy@lab\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m~/ai-security-course-labs/labs/m14\u001b[00m$ "]
